\begin{abstract}
Multimodal emotion recognition reports margins without reporting how much room those
margins occupy. We measure two elementary quantities that set the scale of every such
margin. The
\emph{fusion headroom} $H$ is the gain of a paper's multimodal system over its own best
single modality, read from matched results under the same protocol and model family; the \emph{majority-class
baseline} is the score of a classifier that ignores its input. Reading eight matched
comparisons from five corpora at their sources and measuring two more ourselves on
CMU-MOSI and CMU-MOSEI, we find $H$ between $-1.8$ and $+9.1$ points: only the two
EEG-with-eye-movement corpora exceed $3.22$, the conversational, sentiment and peripheral-physiology benchmarks
stay at or below it, and four of the ten comparisons are negative. Re-measuring MELD with frozen encoders that never saw its
labels gives a headroom of $0.04$ weighted-F1 points against the published $2.81$,
while the text-only score rises by about five. A one-point margin on MELD thus consumes
about one third of the published fusion headroom, whereas with frozen features the
estimated headroom is statistically indistinguishable from zero; a constant classifier
already reaches $31.3$ weighted F1 on the same test split. Collapse toward the majority class raises
weighted F1 in $97.6\%$ of $9{,}261$ random confusion matrices with positive kappa, while
kappa never rises. We therefore recommend reporting the majority-class baseline, fusion
headroom, and at least one collapse-resistant metric alongside the primary metric.
\keywords{Multimodal fusion \and Emotion recognition \and Evaluation \and
Majority-class baseline \and Missing modalities}
\end{abstract}

