\section{Introduction}

Multimodal emotion recognition rests on the premise that text, speech, vision and
physiology carry complementary evidence, so that a system which fuses them should
outperform one that reads a single channel~\cite{poria2019meld,zhao2021}. Progress is
accordingly reported as a margin: a new fusion mechanism, or a new way of coping with
missing channels~\cite{lian2023,zhang2024,li2024}, is judged by how many points it gains
over the previous best. These margins decide which architectures the field adopts and
which robustness claims it accepts.

A margin, however, can be read only against the scale on which it is measured, and two
quantities that set that scale are rarely reported. The \emph{baseline}: MELD's test split is
$48.1\%$ neutral, so a classifier that always answers \emph{neutral} scores $31.3$
weighted F1, and the single-modality and severe-missingness conditions that robustness
papers emphasise report scores a few points above it. The \emph{headroom}: if a paper's
best single modality scores $s^\star$ and its multimodal system $f$, then $H=f-s^\star$
is the observed multimodal gain over the strongest unimodal result within that matched
comparison, and a margin $\Delta$ over a
competitor consumes $\Delta/H$ of it. When a system reaches $60.25$ weighted F1 against a
previous $59.25$ on a corpus where the headroom is $3.22$ points, the improvement
is not one point out of the forty left to a perfect score but one point out of three. We therefore ask a question that
the tables the field already prints can answer: how large is the headroom on the corpora
that multimodal emotion recognition uses, and how close to that baseline do reported systems
operate?

This scaling matters for the interpretation of reported gains. If the headroom is only a
few points, much of the
evidence for new fusion mechanisms consists of differences that are small against the
room available, and a metric that rises when a system collapses onto the majority class
can produce such differences by itself. Both quantities are inexpensive to compute, yet
they clarify whether a reported difference is large relative to the fusion headroom
available in the matched comparison.

In this paper, we measure both quantities at their sources. Our contributions are:
\begin{enumerate}
\item a reading discipline for $H$ (same paper, protocol, model family and evaluation
  setting) and a demonstration that violating the model-family condition multiplies $H$ by $2.7$ on SEED-IV
  (Section~\ref{sec:diag});
\item an audit of ten matched comparisons over seven corpora, eight read at their sources
  and two measured first-hand on CMU-MOSI and CMU-MOSEI
  (Sections~\ref{sec:audit} and~\ref{sec:firsthand});
\item a first-hand re-measurement of a published MELD row with frozen modern encoders,
  in which the text-only score rises more than the fused score relative to the published row
  (Section~\ref{sec:meld});
\item a proof that majority-class collapse cannot improve positive kappa or MCC, nor
  balanced accuracy when it is above its chance level $1/K$, beside the measurement that weighted F1 rises under it in
  $97.6\%$ of cases (Section~\ref{sec:diag});
\item closed-form majority-class baselines and reporting practices built on collapse-resistant metrics, whose
  consequence we show on a missing-modality system of our own
  (Sections~\ref{sec:diag} and~\ref{sec:case}, the latter on synthetic data).
\end{enumerate}
We do not re-run published systems or claim that any published number is incorrect. $H$
as measured here is a property of the systems a paper compares, not an
information-theoretic bound on the corpus; Section~\ref{sec:limits} returns to this.

