\section{Two Diagnostics}\label{sec:diag}

\subsection{Fusion Headroom and the Reading Discipline}

For a paper reporting a multimodal system and its unimodal ablations,
$H = f - \max_m s_m$, where $f$ is the score of the full multimodal system and $s_m$ that
of modality $m$ alone, all read from the same paper under the same protocol, model family
and evaluation setting. A margin $\Delta$ between two systems on that corpus has \emph{utilisation}
$\Delta/H$. The ratio is meaningful only where $H$ is clearly positive: with an interval that
excludes zero where seed variance is available, and as an indicative figure for published
point estimates that report none. Where the interval covers zero, as on our frozen-encoder MELD row, $\Delta$ and $H$ should
be reported side by side instead, since a denominator near zero makes the ratio unstable.

Three requirements govern the reading. \emph{Same paper and evaluation setting}: the
unimodal and fused results must come from the same study and matched experimental setting,
although they may appear in different tables or figures. \emph{Same protocol}: splits,
label sets and metrics must match. \emph{Same model family}: differences between a shallow
unimodal classifier and a deeper fused model confound fusion with model capacity. Zheng et al.~\cite{zheng2019} report on SEED-IV, in one results section, EEG alone
at $70.33\%$ and eye movements at $67.82\%$ (linear SVMs), feature-level fusion with SVMs
at $75.88\%$, and a bimodal deep autoencoder at $85.11\%$. Read against the deep model
$H=14.78$; read within the SVM family $H=5.55$. We report only matched values.

\subsection{The Majority-Class Baseline}

We call this quantity a baseline rather than a floor: nothing prevents a system from
scoring below it, and several in this paper do. Let a constant predictor emit class $k_0$,
whose share of the \emph{evaluation} split is $\pi_0$, among $K$ classes. Then accuracy, weighted F1 and macro F1 are, as fractions,
\begin{equation}
A = \pi_0,\qquad W = \frac{2\pi_0^2}{1+\pi_0},\qquad M = \frac{1}{K}\cdot\frac{2\pi_0}{1+\pi_0},
\end{equation}
since class $k_0$ has precision $\pi_0$ and recall $1$ and every other class has
$F_1=0$. Two assumptions are easy to lose: $\pi_0$ is the evaluation share of the class
\emph{actually predicted}, not the training majority, and macro F1 averages over the
label set, not over the classes observed in a split (a common library default). A
practitioner cannot fit the evaluation majority in advance, so a constant predictor chosen
on training data can score far lower; Section~\ref{sec:firsthand} gives a case where the
two differ by $24$ points. On MELD's test split ($K=7$, $\pi_0=0.481$) the baseline, in
percent, is $W=31.3$, $M=9.3$ and $A=48.1$.

\subsection{Metrics Under Majority-Class Collapse}

Let $T_\lambda$ emit a classifier's output with probability $\lambda$ and the majority
class otherwise. For $\lambda_1<\lambda_2$ we have $T_{\lambda_1}=T_{\lambda_1/\lambda_2}
\circ T_{\lambda_2}$, so $T_{\lambda_1}$ is a garbling of $T_{\lambda_2}$ and, by the
data-processing inequality, carries no more information about the label. Cohen's kappa
and multiclass MCC are strictly increasing in $\lambda$ when they are positive. Balanced
accuracy is also increasing in $\lambda$ whenever it exceeds its chance level $1/K$. Thus,
for above-chance classifiers, none of these metrics can be improved by collapse onto the
majority class (balanced accuracy is affine in $\lambda$, while kappa and MCC have numerator $\lambda D$
with $D>0$ and a positive denominator for $\lambda>0$; the derivations and a
numerical verification script are provided in the accompanying \texttt{theory\_check.py}). Weighted F1 has no such
property. We drew $20{,}000$ row-stochastic confusion matrices over MELD's test prior,
each row from a Dirichlet whose concentration was itself drawn uniformly in $[0.2,4]$ so
that the sample spans sharp and diffuse classifiers, and kept the $9{,}261$ with positive
kappa. Garbling them to $\lambda=0.9$ raised kappa in $0$ cases and weighted F1 in
$9{,}041$ ($97.6\%$; Fig.~\ref{fig:garbling}). The proportion moves by less than a point
when the concentration range is halved or doubled. This is the
phenomenon Powers described~\cite{powers2011}, stated for the operation that severe
missingness approximates.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{fig_garbling.pdf}
\caption{Collapse toward the majority class ($T_{0.9}$) applied to 9{,}261 random confusion
matrices over MELD's test prior with positive kappa. Every matrix moves left (kappa
falls); 97.6\% move up (weighted F1 rises).}
\label{fig:garbling}
\end{figure}

The three collapse-resistant metrics do differ in \emph{sensitivity} near the baseline. Kappa
and MCC share the numerator $\lambda D$, with $D=p_{\mathrm{o}}-p_{\mathrm{e}}$ the gap
between observed and expected agreement, but MCC's denominator vanishes like $\sqrt{\lambda}$, so kappa moves
as $\Theta(\varepsilon)$ and MCC as $\Theta(\sqrt{\varepsilon})$ in the mass $\varepsilon$
predicted away from the constant class. Perturbing the constant predictor on MELD's test
prior by a single sample (one prediction flipped to \emph{fear}, the rarest class) moves MCC by $+0.024$ or $-0.009$ and kappa by $+0.0011$ or
$-0.0004$. Near the baseline MCC is the more responsive instrument and kappa the more stable
one, which is a reason to report both rather than to choose between them.

