\section{Headroom in Published Tables}\label{sec:audit}

We selected corpora spanning the modality combinations the field uses: conversational
text--audio--video (MELD~\cite{poria2019meld}), speech with peripheral physiology
(KEMDy20~\cite{sgmt2026}), EEG with eye movements (SEED~\cite{lu2015},
SEED-IV~\cite{zheng2019}) and EEG with peripheral physiology (AMIGOS~\cite{amigos}). For each we located a paper reporting unimodal ablations beside
the fused system. A row was admitted only if
the unimodal and fused scores share one paper, one protocol, one model family and one
evaluation setting; a
paper whose unimodal rows use a different model family from its fused row is recorded as an
illustration, never as a data point (the SEED-IV deep autoencoder above). All reported values
were verified against the original papers, and the source table or figure and metric of
each row are recorded with the data. Table~\ref{tab:headroom} adds
the two corpora we measured ourselves (Section~\ref{sec:firsthand}).

The sample is purposive, not exhaustive. We fixed the modality combinations first and
then took, for each corpus, a paper that introduces it or is standard for it and prints
unimodal rows beside the fused row. Candidates were excluded when we could not identify a
matched unimodal--fusion comparison satisfying the criteria above. We did not search for papers with
small $H$. Applying the same criteria to a different paper on the same corpus may yield a
different value; we therefore record the source and metric of every row to make the audit
reproducible.

