Across the ten matched comparisons, $H$ ranges from $-1.8$ to $+9.1$ points
(Fig.~\ref{fig:headroom}), with \textbf{four negative values}: the fused system scores below its own best single
channel. The ten rows
are not ten independent experiments, since MELD, AMIGOS and KEMDy20 contribute two each.
The observed ordering does not follow a simple pattern by modality type. EEG with eye movements gives the largest headroom;
EEG with peripheral physiology, which shares the property that neither channel is
derivable from the other, is negative on both AMIGOS dimensions; and speech with wearable
biosignals is negative on arousal. The KEMDy20 authors offer an explanation for their case:
acoustic features already carry most of the arousal information, and electrodermal
activity and inter-beat interval mainly track arousal too. These results suggest that
multimodal benefit depends on complementary label-relevant information rather than merely
on distinct sensing channels.

\textbf{Source verification.} During preliminary extraction, three entries were
mismatched; verification against the original papers corrected all three
downward. The KEMDy20 value had been measured against speech alone rather than the best
single modality; the MELD text score had been assigned to the wrong modality combination;
and the SEED-IV value mixed classifier families. Table~\ref{tab:headroom} reports the
corrected values under the matching criteria of Section~\ref{sec:diag}.

\textbf{Distance to the baseline.} Interpretation is most sensitive when reported scores
lie near the baseline. On MELD's
severe-missingness conditions, single-modality systems report weighted F1 in the low
thirties against a baseline of $31.3$. On AMIGOS, the best single modality reaches F1 $56.4$
against a random baseline of $50.0$ that the paper prints in its own table: $6.4$ points
of signal in total, inside which the fusion comparison of Table~\ref{tab:headroom} takes
place. A margin reported in such a regime can reflect, in part, which system collapses onto
the majority class less completely.

