\section{First-Hand Measurements}\label{sec:firsthand}

\subsection{Protocol}

For three corpora we measured $H$ ourselves under one architecture: a masked
bidirectional LSTM~\cite{hochreiter1997} over the utterances of a video or dialogue, with
a per-utterance linear read-out and packed sequences, as in bc-LSTM~\cite{poria2017}.
We use hidden size $100$, dropout $0.5$, Adam with learning rate $10^{-3}$ and batches of
$32$, and train for at most $50$ epochs with early stopping (patience $8$) on a seeded
$20\%$ of the training videos, with twenty seeds ($0$--$19$). Only the input feature blocks change between
configurations. We evaluate three fusion strategies: early concatenation, late averaging of the
unimodal posteriors, and per-modality encoders concatenated before the read-out (mid).
Both the fusion style and the best single modality are chosen on the held-out training
videos, never on test. As a robustness check, hyper-parameters are also selected per
configuration over hidden $\in\{50,100,200\}$ and dropout $\in\{0.3,0.5\}$. Differences
are paired over seeds and reported with bootstrap 95\% intervals, sign-flip permutation $p$-values and the effect size $d_z$.

\textbf{Reproducibility.} The bi-LSTM is deterministic given the seed on four CPU threads;
the frozen encoders of Section~\ref{sec:meld} run in half precision, inference only, on one
6\,GB GPU. Feature files are pinned by checksum, and every number is recorded with the
script that produced it.

\subsection{CMU-MOSI and CMU-MOSEI}

We used the utterance-level features and speaker-independent splits released with the
bc-LSTM line of work~\cite{poria2017,poria2017b}, pinned to a public repository commit
with per-file checksums: MOSI~\cite{zadeh2016} as binary sentiment ($1{,}447$/$752$
training/test utterances) and MOSEI~\cite{zadeh2018} as three-class sentiment
($16{,}188$/$4{,}614$). Labels and masks are identical across the modality files, so all
rows are matched at the sample level.

