\section{Case Study: Applying the Diagnostics}\label{sec:case}

As an illustrative case study, we apply the diagnostics to a controlled synthetic
missing-modality experiment and compare the resulting interpretation across metrics.
We built a representative missing-modality framework with per-channel evidential heads, a
vacuity-weighted fusion gate and asymmetric distillation from a complete-modality teacher,
and evaluated it on a \emph{synthetic} tri-modal benchmark whose class priors and split
sizes are taken from MELD but whose samples are generated, so that per-channel reliability
is known (ten seeds, seven availability patterns). It achieved $+5.06$ weighted F1 over the distillation baseline in the
visual-only condition, and $+3.90$ over the strongest system there, plain early fusion.
On that benchmark $H=7.3$, so the two margins correspond to $69\%$ and $53\%$ of the
observed fusion headroom; the system scored $36.0$ against a majority-class baseline of
$31.0$ (the generator resamples the priors per
split, so its baseline differs slightly from MELD's $31.3$), and four
of the six systems compared scored below it. Under macro F1, which weights the
rare classes but does not correct for chance, the margin shrank to $+0.66$. A five-seed
re-run that also logged kappa and MCC gives, in the same condition, $+4.82$ weighted F1
against $+2.47$ kappa points (we write kappa and MCC points for $100\times\Delta$). In that
re-run, the comparison against a matched-capacity attention gate remained
positive across all three metrics: averaged over the seven availability patterns, $+2.19$ weighted F1
(paired $t$ over five seeds, $p=0.0095$), $+1.97$ kappa points ($p=0.011$) and $+1.84$ MCC points ($p=0.0077$). This
margin is smaller but directionally consistent across metrics that reward
different properties, making a metric-specific explanation less likely.

%------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Discussion}\label{sec:discussion}

\textbf{Why the headroom is small.} Three explanations fit Table~\ref{tab:headroom}, and
our data cannot yet separate them. The first is redundancy on the labelled dimension:
where channels carry overlapping information about the label, as acoustic and
physiological signals do about arousal in KEMDy20, the best channel already holds most of
what the others would add. The second is weak auxiliary channels: on MOSI, audio and video
alone score below a constant classifier, and on MELD a whole-frame visual encoder is near
the majority-class baseline, so fusion has little to extract from them; work on imbalanced multimodal
learning adds that multimodal and unimodal objectives can conflict during training, which
can leave a fused model short of what even its weaker channels hold~\cite{wei2024mmpareto}. The third is a dominant text
channel: in conversational and sentiment corpora the transcript carries most of the label,
and in our MELD re-measurement the text-only score increases more than the fused score,
shrinking $H$ toward zero. The largest headroom belongs to SEED, where EEG and
eye movements are nearly equally informative on their own ($78.51$ and $77.80$), which is
consistent with the second and third explanations: neither channel is weak, and neither
dominates.

\textbf{Implications for published margins.} Utilisation provides a scale for
interpreting a reported gain. A
one-point margin on MELD consumes more than a third of the headroom in the published bc-LSTM row; on
frozen features, where $H$ is indistinguishable from zero, a one-point gain would exceed the
observed headroom and should not be attributed to fusion without additional matched
ablations. We do not suggest that such margins are wrong. We suggest
that a margin should be reported beside the room it occupies, so that readers can tell a
fusion result from a representation result.

\textbf{Two pitfalls in first-hand headroom.} Even with matched architectures, our
measurements exposed two mechanisms that a single results table does not reveal. Choosing the fusion
style by its test mean inflated the MOSEI gain two- to fourfold (Section~\ref{sec:firsthand}),
and model selection on a held-out split where the released features reach near-ceiling
accuracy (MOSI's $99\%$ against $80\%$ on test) makes selection close to arbitrary. Both issues can
be checked by reporting held-out and test results and by selecting configurations using
held-out data only.

\textbf{Relation to other audits.} Like the audits of standard splits~\cite{gorman2019}
and statistical power~\cite{card2020}, this is a result about reporting; unlike them, its
quantity can be read from tables that papers already print (Section~\ref{sec:audit}).

%------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Limitations}\label{sec:limits}

\textbf{$H$ is a property of systems, not of corpora.} We measure the headroom realised
by the systems a paper compares, not a bound; a better fusion method could enlarge it.
The corpora span eleven years and different model families, but the sample is too small
to support a trend with model capacity. Moreover, weak unimodal representations can
increase the measured $H$, as illustrated by the MELD re-measurement in
Section~\ref{sec:meld}.

\textbf{Sample.} We read one paper per corpus, with two rows each for MELD, AMIGOS
and KEMDy20. The metrics differ across rows (accuracy, weighted F1, UAR and F1), so
values are comparable within a row, not across corpora.

\textbf{Released features.} Our MOSI and MOSEI rows use features whose text and video
extractors are reported to have been trained on the training split: held-out training
videos reach far higher accuracy than test, which saturates model selection. The rows
therefore describe fusion over those released features rather than a dataset-level
estimate of fusion headroom. The MELD measurement avoids this limitation by using frozen
encoders, although it covers only one corpus.

%------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
\section{Recommendations and Conclusion}

We recommend the following reporting practices for authors, reviewers and benchmark
designers. \emph{To authors of fusion and
missing-modality papers}: for each ablation or degradation condition, report three numbers
beside the metric the field expects: the majority-class baseline
$2\pi_0^2/(1+\pi_0)$ from the evaluation label counts; the headroom $H$, matched on paper,
protocol, model family and evaluation setting, with the utilisation $\Delta/H$ of any claimed margin
whose $H$ is clearly positive; and a metric that resists majority collapse (kappa, MCC or
balanced accuracy, the last with chance level $1/K$ rather than zero), reporting kappa and
MCC together near the baseline. \emph{To
reviewers}: two of the three can be checked from the submission itself, the baseline from the
label counts the paper already prints and $H$ from its own ablation table, so a margin that
appears in weighted F1 but does not persist under kappa or MCC, or one that claims most of the
available headroom, can be queried without re-running anything; as a working heuristic, and
not a result of this study, half the headroom is a reasonable point at which to ask where a
gain comes from. \emph{To benchmark designers}: a corpus whose best single channel sits
only a few points above that baseline, as in the AMIGOS comparison, provides limited
dynamic range for separating fusion methods. These practices are in
the spirit of consensus reporting checklists for machine-learning-based
science~\cite{kapoor2024reforms}.

In the matched comparisons examined here, the observed fusion headroom is generally
small; weighted F1 should not be used alone to interpret differences of this scale when
majority-class collapse is plausible; and on the one published row we could re-measure,
using frozen modern representations reduces the observed headroom to a value
indistinguishable from zero. Future work should repeat the first-hand measurements with frozen
modern encoders on MOSI and MOSEI, and ask which corpus designs would make fusion
valuable, which is a question about how emotion is expressed across channels rather than
about architectures.

